      "text": "To find the sum of the \\(x\\)-values that satisfy the equation \\(5 = \\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x+2}\\), we first simplify the right-hand side. We observe that the numerator can be factored as follows:\n\n\\[x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x = x(x^2 - 2x - 8) = x(x-4)(x+2).\\]\n\nThus, the right-hand side of the equation becomes:\n\n\\[\\frac{x(x-4)(x+2)}{x+2}.\\]\n\nFor \\(x \\neq -2\\), we can cancel the \\(x+2\\) terms:\n\n\\[\\frac{x(x-4)(x+2)}{x+2} = x(x-4) = x^2 - 4x.\\]\n\nSo the equation \\(5 = \\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x+2}\\) simplifies to:\n\n\\[5 = x^2 - 4x.\\]\n\nRewriting this in standard form, we get:\n\n\\[x^2 - 4x - 5 = 0.\\]\n\nWe can factor the quadratic equation as follows:\n\n\\[x^2 - 4x - 5 = (x-5)(x+1) = 0.\\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions: \\(x = 5\\) and \\(x = -1\\). Therefore, the sum of the \\(x\\)-values that satisfy the original equation is:\n\n\\[5 + (-1) = 4.\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{4}.\\]",
