    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\(5 = \\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x + 2}\\), we start by performing polynomial long division on the expression \\(\\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x + 2}\\).\n\n1. Divide the leading term of the numerator \\(x^3\\) by the leading term of the denominator \\(x\\) to get \\(x^2\\). Multiply \\(x^2\\) by \\(x + 2\\) to get \\(x^3 + 2x^2\\).\n2. Subtract \\(x^3 + 2x^2\\) from \\(x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x\\) to get \\(-4x^2 - 8x\\).\n3. Divide the leading term \\(-4x^2\\) by \\(x\\) to get \\(-4x\\). Multiply \\(-4x\\) by \\(x + 2\\) to get \\(-4x^2 - 8x\\).\n4. Subtract \\(-4x^2 - 8x\\) from \\(-4x^2 - 8x\\) to get 0.\n\nSo, the result of the division is \\(x^2 - 4x\\). Therefore, the equation \\(5 = \\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x + 2}\\) can be rewritten as:\n\\[5 = x^2 - 4x\\]\n\\[x^2 - 4x - 5 = 0\\]\n\nNext, we solve the quadratic equation \\(x^2 - 4x - 5 = 0\\). We can factorize it as:\n\\[x^2 - 4x - 5 = (x - 5)(x + 1) = 0\\]\nSetting each factor equal to zero gives us the solutions:\n\\[x - 5 = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x + 1 = 0\\]\n\\[x = 5 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = -1\\]\n\nThe sum of the \\(x\\)-values that satisfy the equation is:\n\\[5 + (-1) = 4\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\\boxed{4}\\]",
