      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the sum of the \\( x \\)-values that satisfy the equation \\( 5 = \\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x+2} \\), we first simplify the right-hand side by performing polynomial division.\n\nThe dividend is \\( x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x \\) and the divisor is \\( x + 2 \\). We do the division step by step:\n\n1. Divide the leading term of the dividend \\( x^3 \\) by the leading term of the divisor \\( x \\) to get \\( x^2 \\).\n2. Multiply \\( x^2 \\) by the divisor \\( x + 2 \\) to get \\( x^3 + 2x^2 \\).\n3. Subtract this from the original polynomial: \\( (x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x) - (x^3 + 2x^2) = -4x^2 - 8x \\).\n4. Divide the leading term of the new dividend \\( -4x^2 \\) by the leading term of the divisor \\( x \\) to get \\( -4x \\).\n5. Multiply \\( -4x \\) by the divisor \\( x + 2 \\) to get \\( -4x^2 - 8x \\).\n6. Subtract this from the current polynomial: \\( (-4x^2 - 8x) - (-4x^2 - 8x) = 0 \\).\n\nSo, the quotient is \\( x^2 - 4x \\) and the remainder is 0. Therefore, we have:\n\\[ \\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x+2} = x^2 - 4x + 10. \\]\nThe equation becomes:\n\\[ 5 = x^2 - 4x + 10. \\]\nRearranging terms to set the equation to zero gives:\n\\[ x^2 - 4x + 5 - 5 = 0, \\]\n\\[ x^2 - 4x = 0. \\]\nFactoring out \\( x \\) we get:\n\\[ x(x - 4) = 0. \\]\nSo, the solutions are:\n\\[ x = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = 4. \\]\nThe sum of the solutions is:\n\\[ 0 + 4 = 4. \\]\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{4}. \\]",
